# Sealed capture sets are not rendered as text by `git diff`, `git show` or
# `git log -p`, and GitHub shows them as a binary change.
#
# Not tidiness. On 2026-09-14 the consequence set was merged, and merging it
# meant reviewing it, and reviewing a diff of a corpus means reading the
# corpus: all fifty-six captures were read by the thread that owns parser
# changes, an hour after the set was written to be unseen by exactly that
# thread. Nobody did anything wrong. A reviewer has to check something, and
# the only thing the review surface offered was the text.
#
# Everything a reviewer actually needs -- row count, unique ids, uniform
# columns, the population of every label column, per-block counts against a
# rotation claimed in advance -- is computable without reading a capture, and
# `Tools/CorpusRunner/corpus-shape.py` computes it. This file removes the
# easier, wrong option, so the shape report is what a reviewer reads.
#
# Tools/CorpusRunner/test_score.py fails when a sealed set is missing a rule
# here, or when a rule here covers a file that is not sealed. The list is
# derived from corpus_paths.sealed() but written out, because git reads this
# file and cannot call Python.

Tools/CorpusRunner/adversarial/adversarial.tsv -diff
Tools/CorpusRunner/consequence/consequence.tsv -diff
Tools/CorpusRunner/everyday/everyday.tsv -diff
Tools/CorpusRunner/heldout/heldout.tsv -diff
